Confidence-scheduled verification for DSpark (arXiv:2607.05147, section on
confidence-scheduled verification), enabled with VLLM_DSPARK_VERIFY_SCHED=1.

The DSpark checkpoints carry a trained confidence head (per-slot acceptance
probability from [hidden; markov_w1[previous token]]) that the pinned vLLM
skips. With the variable set, the drafter evaluates it inside the captured
draft step, the running product (prefix survival) rides back to the
scheduler on the ModelRunnerOutput, and the scheduler trims each running
request's draft placeholders to a per-request verification length before
scheduling: VLLM_DSPARK_VERIFY_MODE=greedy (default; maximises tokens
processed per second against the step-time profile in
VLLM_DSPARK_VERIFY_PROFILE plus an online overhead term) or threshold
(VLLM_DSPARK_VERIFY_THRESHOLD on the prefix survival); with
VLLM_DSPARK_VERIFY_UNIFORM=1 the greedy mode gives all decode requests of a
step the same length, which keeps uniform-decode batches on full CUDA graphs.
Unset, the engine behaves as before (the head is not even loaded).

